\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The multiplicity module and conjugation}
\date{2026-11-15}

\p{Kluberg-Stern, Morel, Napoly and Petersson write flavour
\citet{p. 447}{klubergsternmorelnapolypetersson1983flavours}.
Golterman and Smit write flavor
\citet{p. 61}{goltermansmit1984flavor}.
Lee and Sharpe write flavor, including for the matrices built
by complex conjugation of the Dirac matrices, and they call
that choice a convention
\citet{printed p. 18, after (48)}{leesharpe1999flavor}.
\ref{fdip-00PS} calls the multiplicity module the taste
module, as a name for the left multiplicity alone, and only
when the right multiplications that commute with the
counting operator are the scalars. Heller writes flavor,
and places the flavor matrices on the right of the tensor
product and transposes them in the composition
\citet{(3.6), p. 4}{heller1997schrodinger}.
Right Clifford multiplication here uses neither a transpose
nor a complex conjugate. The comparison in
\ref{fdip-009X} makes no taste identification and no
Dirac–Kähler identification.}
